<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint-Jean-Pied-de-Port</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint-Jean-Pied-de-Port is a small town in the French Basque Country, at the foot of the road over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> to Roncesvalles. It was the chief town of Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, the part of the Kingdom of Navarre north of the mountains, and is a major stop on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camino-frances">French Way</a> of the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-jean-pied-de-port.jpg" alt="Saint-Jean-Pied-de-Port" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hayden Soloviev · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint-Jean-Pied-de-Port.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name means foot of the pass in Pyrenean French; in Basque the town is Donibane Garazi. It lies on the river Nive in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basses-pyrenees">Pyrénées-Atlantiques</a> department of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, 8 km from the Spanish border and about 75 kilometres by road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-navarre">Upper Navarre</a>. The region around it is called the Pays de Cize, or Garazi in Basque. The town's layout is essentially one main street, encircled by sandstone walls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The original town at nearby Saint-Jean-le-Vieux was razed in 1177 by the troops of Richard the Lionheart after a siege. The Kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> refounded the town on its present site shortly afterwards. It then belonged to the Kingdom of Navarre and was the seat of the sheriff of the district of Lower Navarre, the merindad of Ultrapuertos or Deça-Ports.</p><p>It remained the seat until the period of the Spanish conquest (1512-1528), when King Henry II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> transferred the seat of the royal institutions to Saint Palais (Donapaleu) on safety grounds. The citadel above the town was remodelled by Vauban in the 17th century, and the Porte St-Jacques, the city gate, was added to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a> in 1998 as part of the sites along the Routes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town stands at the base of the Roncevaux Pass, and the routes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, Vézelay and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-puy-en-velay">Le Puy-en-Velay</a> meet there. It was the pilgrims' last stop before the mountain crossing and is the second most popular starting point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camino-frances">French Way</a> of the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>.</p><p>The cobbled rue de la Citadelle runs from the fifteenth century Porte St-Jacques to the Porte d'Espagne by the bridge, past old houses of pink and grey schist. Nearby stands the 14th-century red schist Gothic church of Notre-Dame-du-Bout-du-Pont, the original of which was built by Sancho the Strong of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> to commemorate the 1212 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a>. The town is a member of the association of the Most Beautiful Villages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Its notable people include Bernard Etxepare, writer of the first printed book in Basque, and the physician Juan Huarte de San Juan (c. 1530-1592).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saint-Jean-Pied-de-Port” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Albret kings kept a small Basque-speaking territory on the northern side around <b>Saint-Jean-Pied-de-Port</b> and Saint-Palais, which Charles V gave up around 1530 as too hard to hold.</blockquote><a href="/state/lower-navarre">Lower Navarre</a></li></ul></section>
